![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `Platform` object is used in the configuration snippet but is not imported. This will cause a reference error if the code is copied directly.

```suggestion
import { Platform } from 'react-native';
import Purchases from 'react-native-purchases';

Purchases.configure({
  apiKey: Platform.OS === 'ios'
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

These four lines repeat the same `awk` logic to normalize scores. This duplication can be avoided by processing all scores in a single `awk` command, which improves both maintainability and efficiency.

```suggestion
read -r norm_cor norm_com norm_qua norm_cla < <(echo "$m_cor $m_com $m_qua $m_cla" | awk '{
    for(i=1; i<=NF; i++) {
        v=int($i/2+0.5);
        if(v<1) v=1;
        if(v>5) v=5;
        printf "%s ", v
    }
}')
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current implementation for calculating the weighted average score involves multiple calls to `awk` inside a series of `if` statements. This is inefficient as it spawns a new process for each calculation. This entire block can be replaced with a single, more efficient `awk` command that performs the entire calculation in one process.

```suggestion
        if [[ -n "$correctness" || -n "$completeness" || -n "$code_quality" || -n "$clarity" ]]; then
			weighted_avg=$(awk \
				-v cor="${correctness:-0}" -v com="${completeness:-0}" \
				-v qua="${code_quality:-0}" -v cla="${clarity:-0}" \
				'BEGIN {
					wsum = 0; wdenom = 0;
					if (cor > 0) { wsum += cor * 0.30; wdenom += 0.30; }
					if (com > 0) { wsum += com * 0.25; wdenom += 0.25; }
					if (qua > 0) { wsum += qua * 0.25; wdenom += 0.25; }
					if (cla > 0) { wsum += cla * 0.20; wdenom += 0.20; }
					if (wdenom > 0) { printf "%.2f", wsum / wdenom; }
				}')
		fi
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

This series of `grep | cut` commands repeatedly processes the same `$parsed` variable. This is very inefficient. A single `while read` loop would be much more performant and readable for parsing the key-value pairs returned by `parse_task_line`.

```suggestion
	local description tags estimate plan_link status logged
	local assignee started completed actual blocked_by blocks verified
	while IFS='=' read -r key value; do
		case "$key" in
			description) description="$value" ;;
			tags) tags="$value" ;;
			estimate) estimate="$value" ;;
			plan_link) plan_link="$value" ;;
			status) status="$value" ;;
			logged) logged="$value" ;;
			assignee) assignee="$value" ;;
			started) started="$value" ;;
			completed) completed="$value" ;;
			actual) actual="$value" ;;
			blocked_by) blocked_by="$value" ;;
			blocks) blocks="$value" ;;
			verified) verified="$value" ;;
		esac
	done <<< "$parsed"
```

