![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The initialization logic incorrectly returns `true` even if the database schema creation fails. This happens because `sqliteExec` returns an empty string on error, and `CREATE TABLE` also returns an empty string on success. This masks critical failures during setup.

```javascript
  const result = sqliteExec(schema, 10000);
  return result !== null;
```

