body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `sqliteExec` function suppresses all error information by returning an empty string on failure. This violates the general rule of avoiding blanket suppression of errors (e.g., `2>/dev/null`), making it difficult to debug syntax errors or system issues. It should at least log the error to `stderr`.

```suggestion
function sqliteExec(sql, timeout = 5000) {
  try {
    return execSync(`sqlite3 "${DB_PATH}"`, {
      input: sql,
      encoding: "utf-8",
      timeout,
      stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "pipe"],
    }).trim();
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(`[aidevops] SQLite execution failed: ${e.stderr || e.message}`);
    return null;
  }
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Avoid suppressing stderr with '2>/dev/null' when sourcing files or executing commands; syntax errors or system issues should remain visible for debugging.
</details>

