body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Using `allViolations.some(...)` for deduplication inside a loop has a time complexity of O(n) for each check, making the overall violation collection O(n^2) in the worst case (where n is the number of violations).

While the number of violations is likely small in practice, using a `Set` to track seen rule IDs is more efficient (O(1) lookup) and can make the code's intent clearer.

You could introduce a `Set` before iterating through the messages to track rule IDs found within this scan.

For example:
```javascript
  const allViolations = [];
  const seenRuleIdsInScan = new Set(); // New

  for (const msg of assistantMessages) {
    // ...
    for (const v of violations) {
      const ruleId = v.rule.id;
      // ... cross-turn dedup

      // Deduplicate by rule id within this scan
      if (!seenRuleIdsInScan.has(ruleId)) {
        allViolations.push({ ...v, msgId });
        seenRuleIdsInScan.add(ruleId);
      }
    }
  }
```

