![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The current logic to skip lines containing currency patterns is too broad. It will cause false negatives by skipping any line that contains a currency-like string (e.g., `$10`), even if it also contains a genuine positional parameter violation (e.g., `echo $1`). This would cause the linter to miss the invalid use of `$1`.

A more precise check is needed to skip a line only if it contains currency-like patterns but no actual positional parameters.

```suggestion
        // Skip lines where `$[1-9]` is part of a currency/pricing pattern (e.g. $28/mo, $1.99)
        // and not a standalone positional parameter.
        if (!/\$[1-9](?![0-9.,/])/.test(trimmed)) {
          continue;
        }
```

