![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The high-entropy token detection logic has a couple of issues:
1.  `text.indexOf(token)` on line 231 is unreliable. If a token appears multiple times, it will always return the index of the first occurrence, leading to an incorrect `index` in the `LeakMatch` object.
2.  The check for already-caught tokens on line 215 (`m.matchedText === token`) is insufficient. A high-entropy token could be a substring of a larger secret already matched by another pattern (e.g., part of a database URL). The check should be based on index ranges.
3.  Splitting the string by delimiters (`.split()`) is less efficient and robust for this task than iterating with a global regex.

I suggest refactoring this block to use `RegExp.exec()` in a loop to get both the token and its correct index, and to perform a more robust overlap check.

```typescript
  // --- High-entropy token detection (catch-all for unknown formats) ---
  // Use a regex to find all potential tokens with their indices to avoid incorrect index from `indexOf`
  const tokenRegex = /[A-Za-z0-9_\-/.+=]{20,}/g;
  let tokenMatch: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((tokenMatch = tokenRegex.exec(text)) !== null) {
    const token = tokenMatch[0];
    const index = tokenMatch.index;

    // Skip tokens that are part of an already-found pattern match by checking index ranges
    const alreadyCaught = matches.some(
      (m) => index >= m.index && index < m.index + m.matchedText.length,
    );
    if (alreadyCaught) {
      continue;
    }

    const entropy = shannonEntropy(token);
    if (entropy >= ENTROPY_THRESHOLD) {
      matches.push({
        patternName: "high_entropy",
        description: `High-entropy token (${entropy.toFixed(2)} bits/char)`,
        matchedText: token,
        index,
        entropy,
      });
    }
  }
